A fresh `git clone` does not carry refs/karr/*, so every karr command in a new checkout answers with "No karr board in this repository: nothing is stored under refs/karr/. ... Run karr sync to fetch the board, or karr init to start one here."

The message is accurate and its advice is right — but karr already has everything it needs to just do it. The remote is configured, and `git ls-remote origin refs/karr/*` answers in well under a second. Making a human (or an agent) run a second command to recover state karr can see from where it stands is a papercut that hits every new checkout, and it hits agents hardest: they read it as "there is no board" and move on.

Proposal: when refs/karr/ is empty locally AND a remote exists AND that remote advertises refs/karr/*, fetch it and continue instead of erroring. Keep the current message for the genuinely empty case (no remote, or remote has no board) — that is when `karr init` really is the answer.

Care needed: this must not turn into an implicit sync on a board that legitimately has zero refs locally after a destroy, and it should stay quiet about what it did (one line to STDERR at most). Also decide whether it is opt-out (KARR_NO_AUTO_FETCH) rather than opt-in.
